{
  "_comment": "The PM agent's vocabulary. Separate from mirag's on purpose: the lexicon is what decides whether a request is asking for code, for a project, or for neither, and an agent reading one subject while classifying questions with another subject's words is the exact way the two would blend. `Lexicon.pattern` raises KeyError on a missing key, so an incomplete file refuses to build a container — that is the completeness check, and it is free.",
  "intent": {
    "_comment": "code_verbs, code_artifacts and every project_* pattern are `(?!)`, which never matches. That makes `asks_for_code` and `asks_for_project` permanently False, so this process cannot take the code branch or the project branch of the pipeline no matter what it is asked. It is a second lock behind process isolation, not a substitute for it.",
    "question_start": "^\\s*(what|which|who|when|where|why|how|is|are|does|do|can|should|could|would)\\b|^\\s*[¿]",
    "code_verbs": "(?!)",
    "code_artifacts": "(?!)",
    "project_scaffolding": "(?!)",
    "project_layers": "(?!)",
    "project_composite": "(?!)",
    "project_companions": "(?!)",
    "symbol_hints": "(?!)",
    "graph_hints": "(?!)",
    "depth_hints": "\\b(in depth|thorough|comprehensive|deep dive|detailed|trade[- ]?offs?|compare|why)\\b"
  },
  "demos": {
    "_comment": "No prepared demos. With the offline lock on, `DemoCatalog` recognises nothing and the answer says there is no model rather than replaying a script — which is the honest state, because none has been written for this corpus yet.",
    "project": "(?!)",
    "repair": "(?!)",
    "code": "(?!)",
    "conceptual": "(?!)"
  },
  "claims": {
    "_comment": "Never matches, and that is a judgement rather than a placeholder. `ClaimAuditor` warns that a claim of passing tests is not backed by an execution; every one of its messages is about tests and markers. This agent runs nothing, so the auditor would either stay silent or emit a warning about test evidence for an answer that never mentioned a test. The equivalent job here — not presenting perceived as measured, not stating a disputed item as settled — belongs to the governance skills, where the corpus's own rules are.",
    "success": "(?!)",
    "already_warns": "(?!)"
  },
  "project_state": {
    "_comment": "Empty shortcuts, so `ProjectStateResponder.answer` always returns None and a question about this agent reaches the model with `state-what-this-knowledge-is` retrieved. The renderers it would otherwise use are written for the backend — where the code is written, which modules exist — and answering those from the PM corpus would be a confident wrong answer. work_verbs still matters: it is what stops a request to DO something being read as a question ABOUT the agent.",
    "work_verbs": "\\b(write|draft|produce|create|build|plan|prioriti[sz]e|scope|assess|analy[sz]e|review|revise|define|specify|decompose|estimate|map|run|design|escrib[ií]|redact[aá]|cre[aá]|arm[aá]|plane[aá]|prioriz[aá]|defin[ií]|revis[aá])\\b",
    "shortcuts": {}
  },
  "model_routing": {
    "_comment": "The experimental router is off by default; these keep it inert if switched on.",
    "architectural": "(?!)",
    "complex": "(?!)",
    "simple": "(?!)"
  },
